Peter came to Jesus and asked him, "Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times."
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a king who wanted to collect all the money his servants owed him.
While the King began to do this, a man who owed him millions of dollars was brought to him.
The man could not pay his debts, so his master ordered him, his wife, his children, and all his possessions to be sold to pay off his debts.
The server fell to his knees before him. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will return everything."
The lady showed compassion for him, forgave him for what he had been, and released him.
But then that valet came out and found one of the other valets to whom he owed a few dollars. He grabbed him and started choking him. 'Pay me what you owe me!' he said.
The other servant fell on his knees. 'Give me time,' he pleaded. 'I will pay you back.'"
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and had the man thrown into prison. The man would be held there until he could pay back what he owed.
The remaining servants saw what happened. They were greatly disturbed. They went and told their master everything that happened.
Then the master called the first servant inside. And he said to him, "You wicked servant, I forgave you all that you owed me because you entreated me.
Didn't I have mercy on the other servant, just as you have shown me mercy?
In a rage, his master handed him over to the jailers. He would be punished until he repaid everything he owed.
Thus, if your father who is in heaven treats you according to your deeds, he will deal with each of you according to your deeds, unless you forgive your brother from your heart.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
