A multitude of people from all the Judean countryside and all the people of Jerusalem were confessing their sins, and John was baptizing them in the river Jordan.
John wore clothes made of camel hair. He had a leather belt on his waist. He ate locusts and wild honey.
This is what John used to preach: "After me comes one who is mightier than I am, whose sandals I am not worthy to untie."
"I baptize you with water. But he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." The baptism and temptation of Jesus.
At that time, Jesus came from Nazareth of Galilee. John baptized him in the River Jordan.
As Jesus was coming up out of the water, at that moment he saw the heavens being torn open and the Spirit descending on him like a dove.
A voice called to him from the sky and said, "You are my son, and I love you. I am very proud of you."
At that time, the Holy Spirit sent Jesus into the desert.
He was in the desert for 40 days. There a devil tempted him. Wild beasts did not harm Jesus. Angels took care of him. Jesus chooses the first disciples.
After they imprisoned John, Jesus went to Galilee. He preached the good news of God.
"The time has come," he said. "The Kingdom of God is near. Turn away from your sins and believe in the Good News!"
One day Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were throwing their nets into the lake. They were fishermen.
"Come, follow me," Jesus said. "I will make you fishers of men."
Meanwhile, they emptied their window and followed it.
Then Jesus walked a little farther. As he walked, he saw Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were in a boat, preparing their nets.
Immediately they left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men, and went after Jesus. Jesus Expels an Unclean Spirit
Jesus and those with him went to Capernaum. When the Sabbath came, he entered the synagogue. And there he began to teach.
People were astonished at his teaching, for he taught them as one having authority, and not as the scribes.
At that moment, a man cried out in their synagogue. He was controlled by an evil spirit. He said,
"Who are you to tell us what to do, Jesus of Nazareth? Did you come to destroy us? I know who you are: the Holy One, the chosen of God!"
"Be quiet!" Jesus said firmly. "Come out of him!"
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Then it came out of him with a cry.
Everyone was amazed and began to say to one another, "What is this? A new teaching! And with authority! He commands even the unclean spirits, and they obey him."
The news about Jesus spread quickly throughout Galilee. Jesus heals many people.
Jesus and those with him went out of the mosque. Immediately they went along with James and John to the house of Simon and Andrew.
The mother-in-law of Simon, who was lying in bed with a fever, was told to Jesus about her.
So he walked over to her. He took her hand and helped her get up. The fever had left her. After that, she started serving them.
That evening, after sunset, the people brought to Jesus all the sick and those possessed by demons.
All the people in the city gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them. They suffered from all kinds of diseases. He also cast out many demons. But he did not allow the demons to speak, because they knew who he was. Jesus prays in a quiet place.
It was very early in the morning, and still dark. Jesus got up and went out of the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. There he prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they cried out, "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered, "Let us go to another place, so that I can go to these nearby towns. I must preach there also. This is why I came."
Fatool Jalil travels. He preaches in their synagogues. He drives out demons. Jesus heals a man who had a skin disease.
A man who had a skin disease came to Jesus. He knelt before him and asked him humbly. He said to him, "If you want to make me 'clean', you can do it."
Jesus was filled with deep concern. He reached out and touched the man. "I am willing to do this," He said. "Be 'clean'!"
The disease left him immediately. He recovered.
Jesus immediately sent him away. The man was given a strong warning.
"Do not tell anyone about this," he said. "Go and present yourself to the priest. Offer the sacrifices that Moses commanded. This will be proof for the priest and for the people that you are 'clean.'"
But the man went out and began to tell it at once. He spread the news to everyone. In this way, Jesus could no longer enter a city publicly. He stayed outside in desolate places. But people continued to come to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
A few days later, Jesus entered Capernaum again. When the people heard that he had returned home,
A large crowd gathered until there was no more room, not even outside the door. And Jesus spoke to them, preaching the word.
Four of those who came were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they couldn't reach Jesus because of the crowd. So they dug into the bowl above Jesus. After that, they lowered the man from above onto a bed.
Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
There were some teachers of the law present. They were reasoning within themselves,
Why does this boy speak like this? He is saying evil things! But only God forgives sins!
From tomorrow, Issa knew what they were thinking, so he said to them, "Why are you thinking like this?"
Is it easier to say to this man, "Your sins are forgiven"? Or to say, "Rise, take up your bed and walk"?
"I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then Jesus spoke to the man who could not walk.
He said: "We tell you, get up. Take your slippers and go home."
The man got up and took his mat. Then he walked away, while everyone was watching. All the people were amazed. They praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this!" Jesus chooses Levi.
Again Jesus went out beside the Sea of Galilee. A great crowd came to him. He began to teach them.
While he was walking, he saw Levi, the son of Alphaeus; Levi was sitting in the tax office. "Follow me," Jesus told him. Levi got up and followed him.
Later, Jesus was having dinner at the home of a tax collector named Levi. Many tax collectors and other sinners were eating with Jesus and his disciples. They were part of the large crowds that followed Jesus.
There were some teachers of the law who were Pharisees. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. They said to his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard this, and said to them, "Those who are in good health do not need a doctor; it is the sick who need one. I did not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance and to follow me." (Jesus is asked about fasting)
The disciples of John and the Pharisees fast. The people came to Jesus and said to Him, "The disciples of John fast, and the disciples of the Pharisees fast. But Your disciples do not fast. Why?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? As long as the bridegroom is with them, they cannot fast.
But a time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them. On that day they will go away without eating.
People do not sew a new piece of cloth onto old clothes. If they did this, the new piece would pull away from the old. This would increase the tearing.
People don't put new wine into old wineskins. If they did, the wine would burst the skins. Then the wine and the skins would both be ruined. No, everyone puts new wine into new wineskins." Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
On a Saturday, Jesus was walking with his disciples in a wheat garden. And the disciples began to pluck the spikes of the ears of corn, and to eat.
They said to Jesus, "Look! Doing this on the Sabbath is against the Law. Why are your disciples doing this?"
He answered, "Have you not read what David did when he and his companions were hungry? They needed food."
That was when Abiathar was the high priest. David entered the house of God and ate the holy bread of the presentation. It was only allowed for priests to eat it. David also gave some to his men.
Then Jesus said to them: "The Sabbath was made for man's sake, and not man for the Sabbath's sake.
So the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath.
Healing on the Sabbath
Again, Jesus entered the temple. There was a man who had a withered and deformed hand.
Some of the Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They were watching him carefully. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath day.
Jesus spoke to the man who had a withered and crooked hand, saying to him, "Rise and stand in the midst."
Then Jesus asked them, "What does the Law say about what we should do on the Sabbath? Should we do good or do evil? Should we save life or destroy it?" But no one answered him.
Jesus looked around him with anger. He was very upset because their hearts were hardened. Then he said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." So he stretched it out, and his hand was like a new one.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus, seeking to kill him. The crowds follow Jesus.
Jesus walked to the Sea of Galilee with his disciples, and a large crowd from Galilee followed them.
People heard about all the things Jesus was doing. Many people came to him from Judea, Jerusalem, the region beyond the Jordan, Tyre, Sidon, and the surrounding areas.
Because of the crowd, Jesus, peace be upon him, told his disciples to prepare a small boat for him. This would prevent people from crowding him.
Jesus healed many people. Therefore, the sick were pressing forward to touch him.
When the people possessed by evil spirits saw him, they fell down before him. The spirits cried out, "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus commanded them to tell no one about him. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up a mountain. He called some people to come to him, and they came.
From among them, he appointed twelve whom he named apostles. From that time on, they were with him, and he also sent them out to preach.
They will have authority to cast out demons.
Jesus named the twelve, and Simon was one of them. Jesus gave him the name Peter.
There was James, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. Jesus gave them the name Boanerges. Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
There was also Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, and James the son of Alphaeus. And there was Thaddaeus and Simon the Canaanite.
Judas Iscariot was one of many. He is the one who handed Jesus over to his enemies. Jesus and Beelzebub
Jesus entered a house. Another crowd gathered. It was so large that Jesus and his disciples couldn't even eat.
His family heard about this. So they went to take care of him. They said, "He is crazy."
Some lawyers came from Jerusalem. And they said, "He is possessed by Beelzebul, and he casts out demons by the prince of demons."
Jesus called the crowd and said to them, "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
If a kingdom fights against itself, it cannot stand.
If a family is divided, it cannot stand.
